---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import ProjectHeader from '../components/ProjectHeader.astro';
import Attenuate from '../components/Attenuate.astro';
import { PROJECTS } from '../data/site';

const p = PROJECTS.find((x) => x.slug === 'atlas')!;
const others = PROJECTS.filter((o) => o.featured && o.slug !== 'atlas');
---

<Base
  title="Atlas — capability delegation for AI agents"
  description="Atlas replaces the shared API key an AI agent uses with a scoped, attenuable capability token bound to its SPIFFE workload identity — verified offline, with no network hop on the authorization path."
  accent="authorize"
  path="/atlas"
>
  <ProjectHeader p={p} />

  <section class="wrap" style="padding-top:1.5rem">
    <p class="eyebrow">Narrow it. Then try to widen it.</p>
    <p style="max-width:56ch;color:var(--text-dim);margin-bottom:1.75rem">
      An agent holding a token can hand a weaker one to the next hop without asking anyone.
      What it cannot do — at all, by construction rather than by policy — is hand out a
      stronger one. Four clicks to see why that matters.
    </p>

    <Attenuate />
  </section>

  <section class="wrap" style="padding-top:2.5rem">
    <p class="eyebrow">Why not just use an API key</p>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        The default way to let an agent call a tool is to give it a key. That key is
        bearer-authority: whoever holds it has everything it can do, for as long as it
        lives, from anywhere. When the agent passes work to a sub-agent it passes the whole
        key, because a key has no smaller version of itself.
      </p>
      <p>
        Workload identity fixes who the caller is; SPIRE issues an SVID that says
        <em>this process, on this node, in this namespace</em>. Capabilities fix what it may
        do. Atlas joins the two: the token names the workload it was minted for, and a
        verifier checks both without calling anything — which matters, because an
        authorization check on the network path is a dependency that fails during exactly
        the incidents you built the agent to handle.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap" style="padding-top:1rem">
    <p class="eyebrow">What is actually interesting about it</p>
    <ul class="evidence">
      {p.bullets.map((b) => <li set:html={b} />)}
    </ul>

    <div style="margin-top:2.5rem">
      <p class="eyebrow">Scope — what this is not</p>
      <p class="note note--scope">{p.scope}</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap" style="border-top:1px solid var(--line)">
    <p class="eyebrow">Elsewhere in the system</p>
    <div class="grid grid--2" style="max-width:62rem">
      {others.map((o) => (
        <a class="card" href={`/${o.slug}`} style={`--c:var(--${o.plane})`}>
          <div class="card__top">
            <span class="card__name">{o.name}</span>
            <span class="badge badge--plane" style={`--c:var(--${o.plane})`}>{o.planeLabel}</span>
          </div>
          <p class="card__line">{o.oneLine}</p>
        </a>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
